            0.1
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.055,
            0.113,
            0.332,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mission LiFE",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Lifestyle for Environment), an India-led global initiative introduced at the United Nations Climate Change Conference in 2021, encourages people to adopt mindful, eco-friendly lifestyles. Traditional practices and ancient texts in India have long recognised that the Earth functions as an interconnected system. The Earth works through the flow of energy and the cycling of matter across spheres. Unsustainable consumption disturbs this balance. By promoting simple habits, such as saving energy and conserving resources, Mission LiFE highlights how the actions of individuals and communities can help build a sustainable future."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.088,
            0.89,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Vehicular emissions react with sunlight to form ground level smog. This also leads to the formation of ground level ozone, which is harmful for health (remember that ozone in the stratosphere blocks UV radiation and is protective of life). These pollutants make city air unhealthy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.172,
            0.89,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While human impact affects all Earth’s spheres, a combination of local actions and global cooperation can help restore the Earth’s natural systems. For example, the Montreal Protocol has started the process of recovery of the ozone layer through global cooperation. However, the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement in which countries were supposed to reduce their CO emissions have been less successful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.307,
            0.89,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Conserving energy and energy resources, switching to renewable energy resources (like solar and wind), planting trees, saving water and practising sustainable farming can help restore the balance. India has planted billions of trees, expanded energy from solar and renewable sources significantly, and also promoted sustainable farming practices. Individuals can also contribute by saving resources (water, food and energy). This can be done by reducing waste, reusing and recycling materials. Together, all these efforts will help maintain the environmental balance on Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.044,
            0.48,
            0.109,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.044,
                0.48,
                0.109,
                0.515
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.486,
            0.307,
            0.505
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.056,
            0.516,
            0.856,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Discuss how human activities increase the concentration of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere. What would you do as an individual to reduce the emission of greenhouse gas?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.238,
            0.576,
            0.387,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "At a Glance"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.605,
            0.865,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The electromagnetic radiation received from the Sun is the primary source of energy on the Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.643,
            0.864,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Most weather processes like evaporation, condensation and precipitation occur in the troposphere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.688,
            0.178,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.084,
                0.688,
                0.178,
                0.909
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.68,
            0.865,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The shape of the Earth, latitude and tilt of the Earth’s axis are primarily responsible for variations in insolation, and hence, for the uneven heating of the Earth’s surface."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.734,
            0.865,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Uneven heating of the Earth’s surface is responsible for the generation of winds and ocean currents."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.219,
            0.772,
            0.864,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Matter and energy are continuously cycled between the living (biotic) and the non-living (abiotic) systems."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.81,
            0.864,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atmospheric oxygen is used in combustion, respiration and oxide formation, and is restored mainly through photosynthesis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.847,
            0.864,
            0.882
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "On the Earth, water, carbon, nitrogen and oxygen continuously cycle between the atmosphere, oceans, land and living organisms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.884,
            0.864,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Biogeochemical cycles make nutrients available to living organisms, sustain life, regulate climate and balance ecosystems."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 19,
